        <!-- SLIDE 06: Low-E -->
        <div class="ht-ftype__slide" data-slide="5">
          <img src="/images/hotel-slider-lowe.jpg" alt="Low-E window film in historic Philadelphia boutique hotel — original single-pane windows with improved thermal performance" class="ht-ftype__bg" />
          <div class="ht-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="ht-ftype__content">
            <div class="ht-ftype__step">06</div>
            <h2 class="ht-ftype__title">Low-E Film.
Warm in Winter. Cool in Summer.</h2>
            <p class="ht-ftype__desc">Old City, Society Hill, and Rittenhouse boutique hotels often sit in buildings with original single-pane windows — architecturally protected, thermally poor. Low-E film (3M Thinsulate CC75) fixes both problems: rejecting solar heat in summer and reflecting warmth back inside in winter. Essentially clear, no impact on the window's appearance, no structural work required.</p>
            <div class="ht-ftype__faq">
              <div class="ht-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="ht-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can Low-E film be installed on historic windows without violating preservation requirements?<span class="ht-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="ht-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes — Low-E film is applied to the interior glass surface with a pressure-sensitive adhesive and leaves no permanent mark on the glass or frame. It does not alter the window's appearance, profile, or structure. For historic properties with preservation requirements, it is one of the only thermal improvements that does not require a Certificate of Appropriateness or any modification to the window unit. We have worked with historic Philadelphia properties and understand how to navigate these requirements.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="ht-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="ht-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What does 3M Thinsulate actually look like on the glass?<span class="ht-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="ht-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Thinsulate CC75 is essentially clear — it adds a barely perceptible neutral appearance that most guests would never notice. It does not create a mirrored exterior look or add visible tint. Outward views from guest rooms remain completely clear and undisturbed. For historic boutique hotels where the authenticity of the original windows is part of the guest experience, this is one of its primary advantages over reflective or tinted solar films.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="ht-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="ht-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How does Low-E film perform in Philadelphia winters specifically?<span class="ht-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="ht-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Philadelphia's Zone 4A climate includes genuine winters — January average lows in the mid-20s°F — and single-pane glass loses heat rapidly through radiant transmission. 3M Thinsulate CC75 has a low-emissivity coating that reflects interior radiant warmth back into the room rather than letting it escape through the glass. The result is warmer, more comfortable guest rooms in winter without any change to the heating system — and a measurable reduction in heating energy consumption from January through March.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="ht-ftype__cta">Get a Low-E film estimate →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

